      <span class="section-num">Section 06</span>
      <h2>Key Findings &amp; Policy Implications</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="section-body">
      <p class="section-intro">
        The following findings are synthesised from the quantitative analysis above. Each is framed for relevance to consumer protection policy, market transparency regulation, and the ongoing review of the UK Fuel Finder open data mandate.
      </p>

      <div class="finding">
        <div class="finding-num">1</div>
        <div class="finding-content">
          <h3>Significant upward price trend across all fuel types (Feb–Apr 2026)</h3>
          <p>E10 rose from approximately 135p/litre in early February to 159p/litre by mid-April 2026 — an increase of ~18%. B7_STANDARD showed a steeper rise from ~141p to ~192p (+36%). These movements, occurring over just 10 weeks, represent a substantial real-terms cost increase for consumers and businesses dependent on road fuel.</p>
          <span class="policy-tag">Policy: Monitor wholesale-to-retail price pass-through rates; publish CMA market update</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="finding">
        <div class="finding-num">2</div>
        <div class="finding-content">
          <h3>Diesel price disparity is the most acute consumer harm risk</h3>
          <p>B7_STANDARD (standard diesel) has an IQR of 33p/litre — more than double E10's IQR of 16p/litre. A consumer at the 75th-percentile diesel station pays 33p/litre more than one at the 25th percentile. For a commercial van with a 70-litre tank, this represents a £23.10 per-fill differential. This disproportionately affects small businesses, hauliers, and rural consumers with limited forecourt choice.</p>
          <span class="policy-tag">Policy: Strengthen price comparison tools; consider regional pricing transparency requirements</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="finding">
        <div class="finding-num">3</div>
        <div class="finding-content">
          <h3>The majority of retailers update prices promptly — but a significant tail does not</h3>
          <p>78.4% of records show a source-to-collection lag under 1 hour, indicating most retailers comply in near-real-time. However, 10.5% of records (5,266) exceed a 24-hour lag, and 3.6% (1,802 records) reflect prices more than 7 days stale. At the station level, 18.7% of forecourts have at least one price record with a lag exceeding 7 days.</p>
          <span class="policy-tag">Policy: Mandate maximum 24-hour update frequency; introduce automated compliance monitoring</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="finding">
        <div class="finding-num">4</div>
        <div class="finding-content">
          <h3>Negative timestamps indicate a systemic feed infrastructure issue</h3>
          <p>2.0% of records (1,011) carry <code>source_updated_at</code> timestamps that post-date <code>recorded_at</code> — a logical impossibility. This is not a data entry anomaly but a systemic pattern suggesting clock desynchronisation between retailer reporting systems and the government ingestion platform. These records cannot be used for freshness analysis and should be treated as unreliable.</p>
          <span class="policy-tag">Policy: Require NTP synchronisation audit of government feed infrastructure; add timestamp validation to API</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="finding">
        <div class="finding-num">5</div>
        <div class="finding-content">
          <h3>Extreme outliers suggest incomplete data validation at the point of entry</h3>
          <p>123 records carry prices outside the plausible range of 50–300p/litre, including values of 0p, 1p, and up to 1,889p. These are almost certainly data entry errors or test records submitted through the government API without adequate validation. Their presence, while numerically small (0.25% of records), undermines public trust in the feed's accuracy.</p>
          <span class="policy-tag">Policy: Implement server-side price range validation (e.g. 70–250p) with rejection/quarantine of outliers</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="finding">
        <div class="finding-num">6</div>
        <div class="finding-content">
          <h3>HVO and B10 coverage is negligible — a gap in green fuel transparency</h3>
          <p>HVO (renewable diesel) is reported by only 54 stations and B10 by only 34 stations in this dataset. As HVO increasingly features in fleet decarbonisation strategies and government Net Zero targets, the near-absence of these fuels from the Fuel Finder scheme means consumers cannot make price-informed low-carbon choices. The government should consider mandating HVO price reporting for stations that stock it.</p>
          <span class="policy-tag">Policy: Extend Fuel Finder mandate to HVO and B10; consider incentives for adoption reporting</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="finding">
        <div class="finding-num">7</div>
        <div class="finding-content">
          <h3>E10 shows the most competitive pricing dynamics of any mainstream fuel</h3>
          <p>With an IQR of just 16p/litre (11% of median), E10 exhibits the tightest price clustering, suggesting effective market competition and strong consumer price-switching behaviour. The Fuel Finder scheme appears to be functioning as intended for this fuel type. Policymakers should examine what structural features drive E10 competitiveness and assess whether they can be replicated for diesel.</p>
          <span class="policy-tag">Policy: Use E10 as benchmark for assessing competitive effectiveness of open data mandates</span>
        </div>
      </div>
